    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Ban Chiang</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rice.jpg" alt="Rice" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Rice <span class="kid-tale-cr">Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 3,000 years ago, farmers made their homes at Ban Chiang, a village in northeast Thailand.</p><p>They grew rice, and one old pot found in a grave still held rice.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cattle.jpg" alt="Cattle" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cattle <span class="kid-tale-cr">dr. Plamen Ivanov, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>They kept water buffalo and cattle, and they hunted wild cattle too.</p><p>Working closely with buffalo in the wet rice fields brought people new illnesses.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>The villagers made their own bronze, a metal made with copper, right in their village.</p><p>They cast bangles, rings, axes, hooks and small bells, but very few weapons.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>In 1966 a student tripped over a tree root and fell onto the tops of buried pots.</p><p>Today Ban Chiang is a World Heritage Site, and its museum shows red-painted pots and graves as they were found.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ban Chiang is a village site in northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, found in 1966, whose graves run from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> farming community to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>.</li><li>Its villagers cast their own bronze in small village settings, without a city or a militaristic state, and their skeletons show active lives with little sign of violence.</li><li>Its age is still disputed: early dates would have made it the world’s earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> culture, but later radiocarbon and Bayesian dating placed its bronze working much later.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The site was found in 1966 when a Harvard student, Steve Young, tripped over the root of a kapok tree and fell onto the exposed tops of buried pottery jars.</p></aside>
    </section>

